The buggy method is `com.google.javascript.jscomp.RemoveUnusedVars.removeUnreferencedFunctionArgs(com.google.javascript.jscomp.Scope)`.

Why:
- All failures have the same symptom: an unused parameter is being removed from function signatures when it should be preserved.
- This happens in SIMPLE optimizations and in tests where only local unused vars should be removed.
- The method explicitly removes trailing unreferenced parameters:
  ```java
  while ((lastArg = argList.getLastChild()) != null) {
    Var var = fnScope.getVar(lastArg.getString());
    if (!referenced.contains(var)) {
      argList.removeChild(lastArg);
    } else {
      break;
    }
  }
  ```
- But its own comment says this should only happen when removing globals is allowed:
  > “we assume that if the user is removing globals, then it's OK to remove unused function args.”
- There is no guard enforcing that policy. So the pass removes unused parameters even when `removeGlobals == false`, which matches the failing tests exactly:
  - `function foo(a){}` becomes `function foo(){}`
  - `window.f=function(a){}` becomes `window.f=function(){}`
  - inner functions similarly lose unused params.

Root cause hypothesis:
- `removeUnreferencedFunctionArgs` is always executed for every function scope from `traverseAndRemoveUnusedReferences`, regardless of whether argument removal is permitted.
- It should be skipped unless the configuration allows it, most likely when `removeGlobals` is true and/or when call-site modification mode is enabled under a compatible optimization setting.

Best fix location:
`RemoveUnusedVars.removeUnreferencedFunctionArgs(Scope)`

